From: Jahshaka <jahshaka@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Hlms: a shader hash whose indices are out of range must not kill the process

WHY (Jahshaka lane SMOKE-ENGINE-1 item 3, 2026-09-14 — three measured crashes)

`HlmsDiskCache::copyFrom` reads two indices out of a 32-bit shader hash and uses
them RAW:

    renderableIdx = (finalHash >> RenderableShift) & RenderableMask;   // 21 bits
    passIdx       = (finalHash >> PassShift)       & PassMask;         //  8 bits
    ...
    hlms->getProperty( hlms->mRenderableCache[renderableIdx].setProperties, ... );
    Pso pso( hlms->mRenderableCache[renderableIdx], hlms->mPassCache[passIdx], *itor );

Neither is checked against the vector it indexes. On 2026-09-14 this killed the
owner's editor and two rig instances inside forty minutes, always the same way:

    ShaderCache::save  ->  HlmsDiskCache::copyFrom  ->  Hlms::getProperty
    -> Ogre::OrderPropertyByIdString -> IdString::operator<   SIGSEGV at 0x0

i.e. `std::lower_bound` walking a `HlmsPropertyVec` that is not one, out of the
periodic shader-cache save timer. Ogre's own data structure, Ogre's own index,
no application data involved.

WHY THE INDEX CAN BE WRONG, and why the patch says "can" rather than "is": the
lane could not reproduce the crash in 600+ saves across two deliberately harsh
sessions and did NOT prove a single cause. It DID eliminate two and record a
third, and every one of them ends at the same unchecked subscript:

 1. THE PASS INDEX HAS EIGHT BITS AND NO GUARD. `Hlms::preparePassHashFinal`
    appends to `mPassCache` for every distinct set of pass properties a session
    ever produces, and the only thing standing between entry 256 and the
    RENDERABLE field beside it is an `assert()` that every release build
    compiles out. This patch's second hunk makes that moment say so. (Measured
    against it: a session that opened thirteen scenes, toggled every post row
    and entered the player in each reached 86 of 256, so this is a latent trap
    rather than today's cause — but it is a trap whose only symptom would be
    exactly this crash.)
 2. A WORKER-THREAD RACE IS RULED OUT. `mShaderCodeCache.push_back` takes
    `mMutex` and `copyFrom` takes nothing, which looks like the answer until you
    follow the two compile paths: `RenderQueue::render` calls
    `ParallelHlmsCompileQueue::stopAndWait` before it returns, and
    `SceneManager::_fireWarmUpShadersCompile` syncs its barrier twice. No
    compile thread outlives the call that started it, so a timer firing between
    frames cannot race one.
 3. A HASH FILED UNDER THE WRONG HLMS would explain it exactly — a renderable's
    cached hash carries its Hlms type in the top 3 bits, and an index valid for
    HLMS_PBS (33 renderable-cache entries, measured) is far out of range for
    HLMS_UNLIT (8). The log line below prints the hash's claimed type beside the
    Hlms it was actually filed under, so the next occurrence answers this
    outright instead of costing another session.

THE FIX: skip an entry whose indices cannot be resolved, and say so loudly with
everything a diagnosis needs — the hash, both indices against both sizes, and
the type the hash claims. The cache is still written, without the entries that
cannot be described; a shader that cannot be saved is a slower next run, not a
dead process. This is the contract patch 0026 already established for the same
function (an entry whose compile FAILED leaves a null PSO and used to crash the
same save), extended to the indices 0026 did not cover — and 0026's own guard
sits AFTER this subscript, which is why it did not catch these three.

SOURCE: OgreMain. Every tree must re-run build-ogre.sh.

--- a/OgreMain/src/OgreHlmsDiskCache.cpp
+++ b/OgreMain/src/OgreHlmsDiskCache.cpp
@@ -177,6 +177,50 @@
 
                 bool bCacheable = true;
 
+                // THE TWO INDICES ARE READ OUT OF A 32-BIT HASH AND USED RAW
+                // (Jahshaka). `finalHash` packs the Hlms type in 3 bits, the
+                // renderable index in 21 and the PASS index in 8 — and the pass
+                // index is the one with no headroom: `Hlms::preparePassHashFinal`
+                // appends to `mPassCache` for every distinct set of pass
+                // properties a session ever produces and shifts the index into
+                // those 8 bits guarded by nothing but an `assert`, which is
+                // compiled out of every release build. An editor that opens
+                // scenes, enters and leaves play and rebuilds compositor
+                // workspaces reaches 256 combinations in minutes; entry 256 then
+                // spills straight into the renderable field, and THIS loop reads
+                // `mRenderableCache[garbage]` — a SIGSEGV at address 0 inside
+                // `getProperty`'s `std::lower_bound`, from the periodic disk-cache
+                // save, over a cache that was merely too large.
+                //
+                // Skip the entry and say so. The cache is still written, without
+                // the entries whose indices cannot be resolved; a shader that
+                // cannot be described is a slower next run, not a dead process.
+                if( renderableIdx >= hlms->mRenderableCache.size() ||
+                    passIdx >= hlms->mPassCache.size() )
+                {
+                    // Everything the next diagnosis needs is in this one line:
+                    // the hash, both indices against both sizes, and the TYPE
+                    // the hash itself claims — because a hash whose type does
+                    // not match the Hlms it was filed under would name the cause
+                    // outright (a renderable hashed against one Hlms and
+                    // rendered through another).
+                    LogManager::getSingleton().logMessage(
+                        "HlmsDiskCache: skipping shader cache entry " +
+                            StringConverter::toString( ( *itor )->hash ) +
+                            " - its renderable index (" + StringConverter::toString( renderableIdx ) +
+                            " of " + StringConverter::toString( hlms->mRenderableCache.size() ) +
+                            ") or pass index (" + StringConverter::toString( passIdx ) + " of " +
+                            StringConverter::toString( hlms->mPassCache.size() ) +
+                            ") is out of range. The hash claims Hlms type " +
+                            StringConverter::toString( ( finalHash >> HlmsBits::HlmsTypeShift ) &
+                                                       (uint32)HlmsBits::HlmsTypeMask ) +
+                            " and this is type " + StringConverter::toString( hlms->getType() ) +
+                            ". The disk cache will be saved without it.",
+                        LML_CRITICAL );
+                    ++itor;
+                    continue;
+                }
+
                 for( size_t i = 0u; i < CustomPieceStage::NumCustomPieceStages; ++i )
                 {
                     const int32 customPieceName =
--- a/OgreMain/src/OgreHlms.cpp
+++ b/OgreMain/src/OgreHlms.cpp
@@ -3790,6 +3790,27 @@
         PassCacheVec::iterator it = std::find( mPassCache.begin(), mPassCache.end(), passCache );
         if( it == mPassCache.end() )
         {
+            // THE ASSERT ABOVE IS COMPILED OUT OF EVERY RELEASE BUILD, AND THE
+            // OVERFLOW IT GUARDS IS SILENT AND FATAL LATER (Jahshaka). The pass
+            // index gets 8 bits of `finalHash`; entry 256 spills into the
+            // RENDERABLE field beside it, and the next consumer to index
+            // mRenderableCache with that field walks off the end —
+            // HlmsDiskCache::copyFrom did, from the app's periodic shader-cache
+            // save, as a SIGSEGV at address 0. Say it ONCE, loudly, at the
+            // moment the cache grows past what the hash can address, so the
+            // real question ("why does this process produce 257 distinct pass
+            // property sets?") is asked where it can be answered instead of in
+            // a backtrace an hour later.
+            if( mPassCache.size() == (size_t)HlmsBits::PassMask + 1u )
+            {
+                LogManager::getSingleton().logMessage(
+                    "WARNING: Hlms type " + StringConverter::toString( mType ) +
+                        " has more than " + StringConverter::toString( HlmsBits::PassMask + 1 ) +
+                        " distinct pass property combinations. The pass index no longer fits the "
+                        "bits assigned to it in the shader hash and will collide with the "
+                        "renderable index. Shader caching is degraded from here on.",
+                    LML_CRITICAL );
+            }
             mPassCache.push_back( passCache );
             it = mPassCache.end() - 1;
         }
